## Claim
Text: "White House coronavirus adviser Dr. Anthony Fauci wrote a 2008 paper that found most 1918-1919 Spanish Influenza pandemic deaths were because of bacterial pneumonia from wearing masks."
Claim author: Dawn Hellewell
Claim date: October 19, 2020
Claim origin: https://web.archive.org/web/20201020155800/https://www.facebook.com/photo.php?fbid=10157592306850779&set=a.10154146794715779&type=3

## Initial Q&A
### Did Dr. Anthony Fauci serve as a White House adviser on the coronavirus (COVID-19) response in October 2020?
Answer: Yes, the search result confirms that Dr. Anthony Fauci served as a White House coronavirus adviser. The France24 article (dated July 2020) describes him as "the United States' leading expert on infectious diseases and White House coronavirus advisor" and notes that he "remained in his position as the administration's coronavirus advisor and continued to warn the president and the public of the dangers posed by the virus." This role was ongoing through October 2020, when the claim was made.

Source URL: https://www.france24.com/en/20200726-anthony-fauci-a-lifeline-for-americans-through-pandemics-and-presidencies-coronavirus

### Did Dr. Anthony Fauci author or co-author a scientific paper that was published in the year 2008?
Answer: Yes. The search result indicates that Anthony Fauci was the "author of another journal article" published in *Emerging Infectious Diseases* (DOI: 10.3201/eid1408.071313), which based on the DOI corresponds to Volume 14, Issue 8, published in August 2008.

Source URL: https://www.newscientist.com/article/dn14458-bacteria-were-the-real-killers-in-1918-flu-pandemic/

### If such a 2008 paper exists, what is its exact title and in which journal was it published?
Answer: Notably, the claim's assertion that the deaths were caused by "wearing masks" is not supported by this paper — the authors concluded that "the majority of deaths in the 1918–1919 influenza pandemic likely resulted directly from secondary bacterial pneumonia caused by common upper respiratory-tract bacteria," attributing this to viral-bacterial co-pathogenesis, not mask-wearing.

Source URL: https://academic.oup.com/jid/article/198/7/962/2192118

### Did Dr. Anthony Fauci author or co-author a 2008 paper that specifically discussed the 1918-1919 Spanish Influenza pandemic?
Answer: Yes, Dr. Anthony Fauci co-authored a 2008 paper that specifically discussed the 1918-1919 Spanish Influenza pandemic. The paper, titled "Predominant role of bacterial pneumonia as a cause of death in pandemic influenza: implications for pandemic influenza preparedness," was published in the Journal of Infectious Diseases in October 2008, with co-authors David M. Morens, Jeffery K. Taubenberger, and NIAID Director Anthony S. Fauci. As the source notes, the researchers "examined pieces of lung tissue preserved from 58 victims of the 1918 pandemic and reviewed reports distilled from thousands of autopsies" to conclude that "the vast majority of influenza deaths resulted from secondary bacterial pneumonia."

Source URL: https://www.cidrap.umn.edu/influenza-vaccines/study-bacterial-pneumonia-was-main-killer-1918-flu-pandemic

### Did the 2008 paper co-authored by Dr. Anthony Fauci conclude that most deaths during the 1918-1919 Spanish Influenza pandemic were caused by bacterial pneumonia?
Answer: Yes. The search result indicates that Dr. Anthony Fauci agreed that "bacterial pneumonia played a major role in the mortality of the 1918 pandemic" and is described as the "author of another journal article out next month that comes to a similar conclusion" as the Brundage and Shanks study, which concluded that "bacterial pneumonia that came on the heels of mostly mild cases of flu killed the majority of the 20 to 100 million victims of the so-called Spanish flu." The cited journal reference is Emerging Infectious Disease (DOI: 10.3201/eid1408.071313), consistent with Fauci's well-known 2008 paper "Predominant Role of Bacterial Pneumonia as a Cause of Death in Pandemic Influenza."

Source URL: https://www.newscientist.com/article/dn14458-bacteria-were-the-real-killers-in-1918-flu-pandemic/

### Did the 2008 paper co-authored by Dr. Anthony Fauci state that bacterial pneumonia was caused by people wearing masks?
Answer: The claim is false. The 2008 article referenced in the search result, in which Dr. Fauci was involved, stated that "bacterial pneumonia played a major role in the mortality of the 1918 pandemic," but the bacterial pneumonia was attributed to secondary infections that followed initial influenza cases—not to mask-wearing. The article explains that "influenza kills cells in the respiratory tract, providing food and a home for invading bacteria," leading to bacterial co-infections, and makes no mention of masks as a cause of bacterial pneumonia.

Source URL: https://www.newscientist.com/article/dn14458-bacteria-were-the-real-killers-in-1918-flu-pandemic/

### Did the 2008 paper co-authored by Dr. Anthony Fauci mention face masks at all as a contributing factor to deaths during the 1918-1919 pandemic?
Answer: No. The search result, which summarizes the 2008 paper by Morens, Taubenberger, and Fauci, does not mention face masks at all. The paper's central finding was that secondary bacterial pneumonia—caused by pathogens like pneumococci, streptococci, and staphylococci—killed most 1918-1919 pandemic victims, but it attributed this to the influenza virus damaging the bronchial and lung epithelium and allowing bacteria from the upper respiratory tract to invade, not to mask-wearing. The claim that the paper linked these deaths to masks is not supported by any information in the source.

Source URL: https://www.cidrap.umn.edu/influenza-vaccines/study-bacterial-pneumonia-was-main-killer-1918-flu-pandemic

### According to the 2008 paper, what was the actual mechanism by which bacterial pneumonia developed in victims of the 1918-1919 pandemic?
Answer: According to the 2008 Morens, Taubenberger, and Fauci paper, bacterial pneumonia developed through a process of viral-bacterial copathogenesis: the influenza virus damaged the bronchial and bronchiolar epithelial cells, breaking down the mucociliary barrier and creating both "a direct pathway for secondary bacterial spread and an environment (cell necrosis and proteinaceous edema fluid) favorable to bacterial growth." The paper describes how the virus's "rapidly spreading epithelial cytolytic infection of the entire bronchial tree" led to "the secondary spread of enormous numbers of bacteria along the denuded bronchial epithelium," with common upper respiratory-tract bacteria (predominantly pneumococci, streptococci, and staphylococci) then causing secondary pneumonia. Notably, the paper attributes the bacterial pneumonia to secondary infection following viral damage to the respiratory epithelium—not to mask-wearing, as the original claim suggests.

Source URL: https://academic.oup.com/jid/article/198/7/962/2192118

### Who were the co-authors of the 2008 paper, and what was Dr. Fauci's role (lead author, co-author, contributor)?
Answer: According to the search result, the 2008 paper "Predominant role of bacterial pneumonia as a cause of death in pandemic influenza: implications for pandemic influenza preparedness" (J. Infect. Dis. 198:1-9) was authored by "David M. Morens, Jeffery K. Taubenberger, and Anthony S. Fauci." Dr. Fauci was listed as the third (last) author, with David M. Morens serving as the lead author and Jeffery K. Taubenberger as the second author.

Source URL: https://www.msubillings.edu/lectures/ww1/pdf/Lewis_WWI_Lecture.pdf

## Final Judgement
## Summary of Key Findings

The evidence confirms that Dr. Anthony Fauci did serve as a White House coronavirus adviser in October 2020, and he did co-author (as third/last author with Morens and Taubenberger) a 2008 paper titled "Predominant role of bacterial pneumonia as a cause of death in pandemic influenza: implications for pandemic influenza preparedness," published in the Journal of Infectious Diseases. The paper did conclude that most 1918-1919 pandemic deaths resulted from secondary bacterial pneumonia. However, the paper explicitly attributed this bacterial pneumonia to viral-bacterial co-pathogenesis—specifically, the influenza virus damaging the bronchial and lung epithelium, which broke down the mucociliary barrier and allowed common upper respiratory bacteria (pneumococci, streptococci, staphylococci) to invade. The paper made no mention of masks whatsoever as a contributing factor to deaths.

## Information Still Lacking

No additional information is needed. The evidence is clear and comprehensive on all relevant points.

## Conclusion

The claim contains a critical false element. While it is true that Fauci co-authored a 2008 paper concluding that most 1918-1919 pandemic deaths were caused by bacterial pneumonia, the claim's assertion that this bacterial pneumonia was caused by "wearing masks" is entirely fabricated. The paper attributed the bacterial pneumonia to viral damage of respiratory epithelium enabling secondary bacterial infection—not to mask-wearing. The paper does not mention masks at all. This is a clear and explicit misrepresentation of the paper's findings.

